<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#piracy">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piracy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as pirates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piracy is robbery and violence committed at sea. Along the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> it was a persistent problem, often linked with smuggling and illicit trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> conducted campaigns against coastal pirates, known in Chinese sources as wokou, as part of its maritime and defensive policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piracy.jpg" alt="Piracy" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WarX, edited by Manuel Strehl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Edward_England.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Piracy is robbery or criminal violence by ship or boat-borne attackers upon another ship or a coastal area, typically to steal cargo and valuables or to take hostages. It is a specific crime under customary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> and of many states' law. It is usually held apart from privateering, which a state authorised by a letter of marque and reprisal against an enemy's merchant ships. The line was not always clear, since whether a man was a privateer or a pirate could depend on whose custody he fell into. Narrow channels that funnel shipping into predictable routes, such as the waters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, have long offered opportunities for it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest documented piracy is that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, who threatened ships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> civilisations in the fourteenth century BCE. Phoenicians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> and Tyrrhenians were known as pirates in antiquity, and in the pre-classical era the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> accepted piracy as a way of making a living, with the abduction of women and children for sale into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> common. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a> it was looked on as a disgrace. The English word comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> pirata and Greek peiratēs, which means anyone who attempts something.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, the merchant Chang Pogo, angered that Silla subjects fell victim to coastal pirates in the unstable late Tang period, petitioned King Heungdeok for a garrison. In 828 the Cheonghae Garrison was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wando">Wando</a> island with an army of 10,000, and Chang became the arbiter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> commerce. The Sindhi Bawarij, active between 251 and 865, looted two treasure ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, which became the casus belli for the Umayyad conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The classic era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> piracy lasted from about 1650 to the mid-1720s, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the United Provinces built colonial empires and seaborne trade grew. Pirate crews divided plunder under a hierarchy in which the quartermaster could veto the captain, and the loot was more often food, water, weapons and ship's gear than buried treasure. Caught pirates were usually hanged, in England often at Execution Dock on the Thames.</p><p>The two-volume A General History of the Pyrates, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1724, gave the public its picture of the Golden Age of Piracy and inspired later fiction, notably Treasure Island in 1883 and Peter Pan in 1911.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piracy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eumelus then kept a fleet that cleared the Black Sea of <b>pirates</b> from the Caucasian coast and the Crimean mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…with Rome, and according to Tacitus, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, Antiochus III, Aristonicus, the <b>pirates</b> and Mithridates VI of Pontus; Claudius responded by remitting their tribute for five years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Water Chenla, on the Mekong Delta, had the river's wealth but was more exposed; late in the century it faced war from Javanese <b>pirates</b> who took the delta and then Chenla itself, and it came to depend on the Shailendra dynasty of Java and Srivijaya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 3rd century Frankish <b>pirates</b> ravaged the coasts of Gaul and Britain; Carausius was given command of the Channel fleet against Frankish and Saxon raiders around 285, and a famous story tells of Frankish prisoners settled by Probus on the Black Sea who seized ships and sailed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Middle Ages its ships fought <b>pirates</b> on the North Sea; in 1401 a Hamburg fleet captured leaders of the Victual Brothers, and Klaus Störtebeker and his men were executed in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>But Red Turban rebels invaded in 1359 and 1361, briefly taking Kaegyong, Japanese <b>pirates</b> ravaged the coasts, and Gongmin was murdered in 1374.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
